└── MQTT client for publishing sensor telemetry to a broker
    ├── Candidates : paho.mqtt.client, aiomqtt, gmqtt, amqtt
    ├── Used APIs  : Client, connect, publish
    ├── Pitfalls   : paho-mqtt is synchronous/callback-based; aiomqtt, gmqtt, and amqtt are async (asyncio-based), requiring the calling code 
    │   (publish_reading/publish_batch) to become async or be wrapped; aiomqtt uses async context managers for connection lifecycle instead of explicit 
    │   connect()/disconnect() calls; gmqtt uses an event/callback registration model (on_connect, on_publish) rather than a direct blocking publish() call 
    │   with a return object exposing .rc; amqtt's publish/connect API and QoS handling differ from paho's MQTTMessageInfo return object, so the getattr(info, 
    │   'rc', 0) pattern would need rework; Dependency-injected client in TelemetryBridge assumes a synchronous object with .publish()/.connect() methods; 
    │   switching to any async client breaks this interface without an async/sync adapter
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── paho-mqtt is used here as a synchronous, callback-based MQTT client to connect to a broker and publish telemetry payloads. aiomqtt, gmqtt, and amqtt 
        are all MQTT client libraries capable of the same core function (connect + publish to a broker) and are explicitly called out in the file's own 
        docstring as the intended substitution target, confirming genuine functional overlap for this role.

